    "contentHtml": "<p><strong>What a Morning Walk Taught Me About Colour</strong></p><p><strong>That No Art Class Ever Did</strong></p><p>The most powerful colour education available to any artist costs nothing and is outside your front door right now.</p><p>I walk the same route most mornings near my home. I have walked it in every season. In sun and rain and the particular grey light of a German November.</p><p>And every single time the colour is different.</p><p><strong>What books teach vs what walks teach</strong> </p><p>Books teach you colour theory. Walks teach you colour reality. A leaf is not one green. It contains ochre at the base, blue in the shadow, yellow where the light hits it directly. That complexity is what makes botanical painting extraordinary — and you can only see it by looking closely at the actual thing.</p><p><strong>How to look differently</strong> </p><p>Slow down to half your usual pace. Look for the unexpected colour first — the purple in what should be a green leaf, the warmth at the base of a white flower. These are the colours nobody sees and that make paintings unforgettable when they appear.</p><p><strong>What to bring</strong> </p><p>Your phone for close-up photographs. A small notebook for colour notes made on the spot. Nothing else required.</p><p><strong>The three colours I discovered on walks that changed my palette</strong> </p><p>The blue in a green shadow. The warmth at the base of every stem. The fact that white flowers are never actually white.</p><p>Go outside today. Find one thing that is a different colour than you expected.</p><p>That is where your next painting begins</p>"
