    "contentHtml": "<p><strong>Why I Paint at My Kitchen Table</strong></p><p><strong>And Why You Should Too</strong></p><p>You do not need a studio. You need a surface, a window and the decision to begin.</p><p>I paint at a kitchen table in Frankfurt. I clear it before dinner. I set it up again the next morning. Every painting I am proud of was made on that table.</p><p>The studio myth is the most expensive creative lie there is. It convinces people to wait — for more space, a better setup, the right conditions — while the paintings they could be making sit unmade inside them.</p><p><strong>What you actually need</strong> </p><p>A tray that holds your supplies. Two water jars. A good window. Two minutes to set up. Two minutes to clear away.</p><p>That is it. That is the entire studio.</p><p><strong>The secret the myth hides</strong> </p><p>The artists who improve are not the ones with the best spaces. They are the ones who show up most consistently. A kitchen table you paint at every day is worth more than a dedicated studio you visit twice a month.</p><p><strong>The one thing that changes everything</strong> </p><p>Make the setup take less than two minutes. Remove every possible reason to delay. The moment the friction disappears the practice begins.</p><p>Your kitchen table is already a studio. You just have to sit down at it.</p><p><em>Ready to set up your space and finally begin? </em></p><p><em>My free guide — Your Creative Space — has everything you need</em></p>"
