    "summary": "Why Negative Space Is the Most Powerful ToolYou Are Probably Not UsingMost beginner painters fill the page.It is a completely understandable impulse....",
    "contentHtml": "<p><strong>Why Negative Space Is the Most Powerful Tool</strong></p><p><strong>You Are Probably Not Using</strong></p><p>Most beginner painters fill the page.</p><p>It is a completely understandable impulse. You have a beautiful sheet of paper and a beautiful subject and you want to use every inch of both. More paint feels like more effort. More effort feels like more value.</p><p>But the paintings that stop people — the ones they cannot look away from — are almost never the fullest ones.</p><p><strong>What negative space actually is</strong> </p><p>The unpainted areas of a painting. The white paper that remains. The air around a botanical subject. The silence between marks.</p><p>It is not emptiness. It is a colour. It is a decision. It is often the most important thing on the page.</p><p><strong>What it does to the viewer</strong> </p><p>It gives the eye somewhere to rest. It makes the painted elements appear more considered and more valuable. It creates the breathing room that makes a painting feel calm rather than crowded.</p><p>A painting with generous negative space asks the viewer to slow down. And a viewer who slows down in front of your work is a viewer who feels something.</p><p><strong>The rule I follow</strong> </p><p>Before I begin any painting I decide how much of the paper I want to remain untouched at the end. I give it a number. At least forty percent. Then I treat that commitment as seriously as any other compositional decision.</p><p><strong>Try this today</strong> </p><p>Paint your usual subject occupying only one third of the page. Leave the rest completely untouched. It will feel wrong immediately.</p><p>Live with it for a day. Look at it again tomorrow.</p><p>Notice what happened to the painting overnight.</p><p>Nothing changed. And everything did. </p>"
