    "contentHtml": "<p><strong>Why Being Self-Taught Is a Strength</strong></p><p><strong>Not a Gap You Need to Fill</strong></p><p>Every time someone asks where I studied I feel a small familiar flinch.</p><p>Nowhere. I studied nowhere. I taught myself at a kitchen table in Frankfurt with a beginner's brush set and a very patient sheet of paper.</p><p>For a long time I apologised for that. I no longer do.</p><p><strong>What formal training gives you</strong> </p><p>Structure. Vocabulary. Foundational technique. Connections. These are real and valuable things.</p><p><strong>What it takes away</strong> </p><p>The permission to be wrong. The freedom to develop strange habits that turn out to be your style. The blissful ignorance of rules you might have followed too carefully if you had known them.</p><p><strong>What self-teaching forces you to develop</strong> </p><p>Resourcefulness. Genuine curiosity. The habit of looking closely at work you admire and asking not just what it is but how it was made and why it works. These are skills that no classroom produces. They can only come from necessity.</p><p><strong>The thing nobody says out loud</strong> </p><p>Some of the most distinctive artistic voices working today are self-taught. Not despite their lack of formal training. Because of it. Their work does not look like it came from a curriculum. It looks like it came from a specific person.</p><p>That specificity is the thing that makes art worth looking at.</p><p><strong>What this means for you</strong> </p><p>If you have been waiting to begin until you have taken a class or found a teacher or felt qualified enough — stop waiting. Begin now with what you have and what you know.</p><p>The practice is the education. It has always been</p>"
